  "grammar": {
    "title": "Choosing between gerunds and infinitives",
    "explanation": "A **gerund** is verb + **-ing**. We use it **after a preposition**, like **of**, **about**, or **at**. A proud mother is never shy **about asking** questions.\n\nWe also use a gerund **after some verbs**: enjoy, finish, mind, avoid, keep, miss. A cheerful friend enjoys **sharing** good news.\n\nAn **infinitive** is **to + verb**. We use it **after an adjective** — after easy, hard, good, or difficult. It is hard **to stay confident** in a new place.\n\nWe use the infinitive **to show purpose**, the reason we do something. A lonely student joins a club **to find** new friends.\n\nWe use the infinitive **after some verbs**: want, need, decide, hope, learn. A grateful person decides **to thank** everyone.\n\nAfter **make** and **let** we use the infinitive **without to**. A calm teacher makes her class **laugh**.\n\nSome verbs change meaning. **Try to be** on time means make an effort. **Try being** patient means see if you like it. Stay calm, do not be impatient with yourself, and never feel embarrassed about asking questions. A sensitive friend will always understand.",
    "examples": [
      "She is proud *of helping* her shy brother. (gerund after a preposition)",
      "I enjoy *reading* cheerful stories before bed. (gerund after enjoy)",
      "It is easy *to stay calm* in this quiet room. (infinitive after an adjective)",
      "He studies every day *to become* more confident. (infinitive to show purpose)",
      "I decided *to be* more grateful this year. (infinitive after decide)",
      "They let me *go* home early yesterday. (let + infinitive without to)"
    ],
    "practice": [
      {
        "q": "Fill in: She finished ___ (read) the book.",
        "a": "reading — finish takes a gerund (verb + -ing)."
      },
      {
        "q": "Fill in: I hope ___ (see) you soon.",
        "a": "to see — hope takes the infinitive (to + verb)."
      }
    ],
